**assistant**:
> You’re right to be suspicious; my previous use of `csplit` was too naive, and your `"1 3 3 2"` example is a clear sign you’re in a *multiway* factor split, not a simple left/right (binary) one.
> 
> Given how much `rpart`’s internals vary across cases, the most reliable and simple approach—especially if you just want the *two groups under the first split* and their means—is:
> 
> **Don’t decode `splits`/`csplit` at all.**  
> Instead, use the model to predict which side of the split each observation goes to, then compute the group means directly.
> 
> This works for:
> 
> - numeric or factor predictors
> - binary or multiway factor splits
> - any `rpart` version, without worrying about `ncat`, `csplit` encodings, etc.
> 
> ### Strategy
> 
> 1. Identify the root split variable from `mod$frame$var[1]`.
> 2. Use `predict(mod, newdata, type = "where")` to get the terminal node for each observation.
> 3. Find which terminal nodes descend from the left child of the root, and which from the right child.
> 4. Aggregate your response `y` over those sets of observations to get the two “sides” and their mean values.
> 5. Optionally, record the split variable name.
> 
> This gives you exactly what you originally asked for: *“the two sides of the first split … along with their average value”*—without relying on fragile internal encodings.
> 
> ### Implementation
> 
> ```r
> library(rpart)
> 
> # data: the data.frame used to fit the model (or an equivalent one)
> # yvar: the name of the response variable as a string
> get_first_split_groups <- function(mod, data, yvar) {
>   fr <- mod$frame
>   
>   # 1. root variable
>   split_var <- as.character(fr$var[1])
>   if (split_var == "<leaf>") {
>     stop("Tree has no splits: root is a leaf.")
>   }
>   
>   # 2. identify left and right children of root
>   # By rpart convention: rownames(fr) are node numbers (1=root, 2=left, 3=right, etc.)
>   node_ids <- as.integer(rownames(fr))
>   root_id  <- 1L
>   left_id  <- 2L
>   right_id <- 3L
>   
>   if (!(left_id  %in% node_ids && right_id %in% node_ids)) {
>     stop("Cannot find left/right children of the root in frame.")
>   }
>   
>   # 3. terminal node for each observation
>   where <- predict(mod, newdata = data, type = "where")
>   # 'where' is a vector of node numbers (terminal nodes)
>   
>   # 4. which terminal nodes descend from left/right child?
>   # Any node whose number is in the subtree of left_id has that node number starting with same prefix
>   # In rpart's numbering: children of node k are 2k and 2k+1; so any descendant of 2 has binary prefix of 2.
>   # An easier practical approach:
>   left_subtree  <- node_ids[node_ids >= left_id  & node_ids < right_id * 2]
>   right_subtree <- node_ids[node_ids >= right_id & (node_ids < left_id * 4 | TRUE)]  # generous upper bound
>   
>   # Filter to terminal nodes only
>   terminal_ids <- node_ids[fr$var == "<leaf>"]
>   left_terminals  <- intersect(left_subtree,  terminal_ids)
>   right_terminals <- intersect(right_subtree, terminal_ids)
>   
>   # 5. assign each observation to left/right side
>   left_idx  <- where %in% left_terminals
>   right_idx <- where %in% right_terminals
>   
>   # Sanity: some trees may be degenerate
>   if (!any(left_idx))  warning("No observations assigned to left side of first split.")
>   if (!any(right_idx)) warning("No observations assigned to right side of first split.")
>   
>   y <- data[[yvar]]
>   
>   left_mean  <- mean(y[left_idx],  na.rm = TRUE)
>   right_mean <- mean(y[right_idx], na.rm = TRUE)
>   
>   list(
>     variable = split_var,
>     left = list(
>       variable  = split_var,
>       group     = "left",
>       mean_y    = left_mean,
>       n         = sum(left_idx)
>     ),
>     right = list(
>       variable  = split_var,
>       group     = "right",
>       mean_y    = right_mean,
>       n         = sum(right_idx)
>     )
>   )
> }
> 
> first_split_df <- function(mod, data, yvar) {
>   x <- get_first_split_groups(mod, data, yvar)
>   data.frame(
>     variable        = x$variable,
>     left.group      = x$left$group,
>     left.value      = x$left$mean_y,
>     left.n          = x$left$n,
>     right.group     = x$right$group,
>     right.value     = x$right$mean_y,
>     right.n         = x$right$n,
>     stringsAsFactors = FALSE
>   )
> }
> ```
> 
> Usage in your loop:
> 
> ```r
> mod <- rpart(y ~ trade.method + trade.method.suffix + persist + solow.conf,
>              data = country_df)
> 
> res <- first_split_df(mod, data = country_df, yvar = "y")
> res
> ```
> 
> This will give you, per country, something like:
> 
> ```text
>   variable left.group left.value left.n right.group right.value right.n
> 1 persist      left  -0.0258      40      right    -0.0397      171
> ```
> 
> You lose the *human-readable condition string* (e.g., `"persist < 0.18"`), but you gain robustness across:
> 
> - numeric vs factor
> - binary vs multiway splits
> - quirks in `csplit` encoding.
> 
> If you **really** need the explicit condition (cut value or level lists), I can help debug that with an example `str(mod)` and `mod$csplit`/`mod$splits` for one case (say `AGO` where you saw `"1 3 3 2"`), but for most analysis tasks, this “predict-then-aggregate” route is safer and simpler.
